1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,872
Twenty-four volts DC, six switches,

2
00:00:02,912 --> 00:00:07,633
and each motor terminal can only be
connected to plus or to minus.

3
00:00:07,673 --> 00:00:11,891
Here's phase a's terminal voltage for our
reference

4
00:00:11,931 --> 00:00:15,881
motor at 3000 rpm: nothing but 24 V
pulses.

5
00:00:15,921 --> 00:00:20,240
Yet the currents are 10 A sine waves, with
ripple riding on top.

6
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:24,393
Today: the three-phase inverter, how it
makes sine waves from pulses,

7
00:00:24,433 --> 00:00:27,483
and what its limits and imperfections
cost.

8
00:00:28,594 --> 00:00:31,155
First, the answers from Lesson 16.

9
00:00:31,195 --> 00:00:34,525
One: six conduction states per electrical
cycle.

10
00:00:34,565 --> 00:00:40,865
Two: 60 electrical degrees per step is 15
mechanical degrees on our 8-pole motor.

11
00:00:40,905 --> 00:00:45,859
Three: six-step puts two terminals
straight across the bus,

12
00:00:45,899 --> 00:00:51,277
a 24 V line-to-line peak. Sine-triangle
PWM reaches only 20.8 V,

13
00:00:51,317 --> 00:00:54,695
√3/2 of that. Today we'll see why.

14
00:00:55,795 --> 00:00:59,424
Start with one leg: two transistors in
series across the bus.

15
00:00:59,464 --> 00:01:02,110
The terminal between them is the pole.

16
00:01:02,150 --> 00:01:06,022
Upper switch on: the pole sits at 24 V.
Lower switch on:

17
00:01:06,062 --> 00:01:10,544
zero, the negative rail N. Never both:
that shorts the bus.

18
00:01:10,584 --> 00:01:16,804
Switch every 50 µs with duty d, and the
pole's average is d times the bus voltage.

19
00:01:16,844 --> 00:01:21,235
The motor follows that average: its
inductance limits how fast the

20
00:01:21,275 --> 00:01:25,865
current can change, so in one period it
moves by at most 2.8 A.

21
00:01:25,905 --> 00:01:30,604
The upper gate drive rides on the pole, so
it's powered by a bootstrap capacitor,

22
00:01:30,644 --> 00:01:34,537
recharged through a diode whenever the
lower switch is on.

23
00:01:35,652 --> 00:01:39,097
Three legs, two positions each: eight
switching states.

24
00:01:39,137 --> 00:01:43,708
Write a state as S_a S_b S_c, with 1 for
upper on.

25
00:01:43,748 --> 00:01:50,563
Its space vector is two-thirds of the bus,
times S_a + a S_b + a² S_c.

26
00:01:50,603 --> 00:01:54,614
State 100 points along phase a, with 16 V.

27
00:01:54,654 --> 00:02:00,704
State 110 sits 60° further on, and so on
around: a hexagon.

28
00:02:00,744 --> 00:02:06,774
The other two, 000 and 111, tie all three
terminals to one rail: zero vectors.

29
00:02:06,814 --> 00:02:12,528
Anything between the eight vectors is an
average over a PWM period.

30
00:02:13,628 --> 00:02:15,743
Which voltage does each winding feel?

31
00:02:15,783 --> 00:02:20,046
We measure poles from the negative rail,
but the star point floats.

32
00:02:20,086 --> 00:02:25,037
Each pole voltage is the phase voltage
plus the star point's. Add all three:

33
00:02:25,077 --> 00:02:30,166
with no neutral, the currents sum to zero,
and so do balanced back-EMFs.

34
00:02:30,206 --> 00:02:33,272
So the star point sits at the average of
the poles,

35
00:02:33,312 --> 00:02:37,630
the common-mode voltage. Phase voltage is
pole minus common mode.

36
00:02:37,670 --> 00:02:43,334
In one period here, the common mode climbs
0, 8, 16, 24 V and back;

37
00:02:43,374 --> 00:02:48,756
the phase voltage takes 0, 8 and 16; line-
to-line, 0 or 24.

38
00:02:48,796 --> 00:02:50,910
The motor never sees the common mode,

39
00:02:50,950 --> 00:02:55,524
though a third harmonic in the back-EMF
would move the star point too.

40
00:02:56,624 --> 00:02:58,882
To set the duties, compare each reference

41
00:02:58,922 --> 00:03:01,749
with a triangular carrier, slowed down
here.

42
00:03:01,789 --> 00:03:06,632
Digital timers sample the reference once
per period. While it's above the carrier,

43
00:03:06,672 --> 00:03:11,082
the upper switch is on, so duty is one
plus the reference, over two.

44
00:03:11,122 --> 00:03:16,475
With references m cos θ, 120° apart, each
pole averages half the bus

45
00:03:16,515 --> 00:03:22,427
plus m times half the bus times the
cosine. The offset is common mode.

46
00:03:22,467 --> 00:03:26,848
So the phase voltage is m times 12 V.

47
00:03:26,888 --> 00:03:33,940
At 3000 rpm and 10 A, our motor needs 8.59
V: m = 0.716.

48
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:38,812
Past m = 1 the references clip: 12 V per
phase,

49
00:03:38,852 --> 00:03:45,158
and √3/2 × 24 = 20.8 V line-to-line.
Lesson 16's number.

50
00:03:46,289 --> 00:03:50,189
Now in frequency, computed exactly from
the switching edges.

51
00:03:50,229 --> 00:03:53,677
The pole voltage has the 200 Hz
fundamental,

52
00:03:53,717 --> 00:03:58,119
a 10.8 V line at the 20 kHz carrier, and
sidebands.

53
00:03:58,159 --> 00:04:03,131
Line-to-line, the carrier line vanishes:
it's the same in all three legs,

54
00:04:03,171 --> 00:04:07,720
common mode again. The sidebands, 400 Hz
either side, remain.

55
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:10,370
Against 6.3 Ω of reactance at 20 kHz,

56
00:04:10,410 --> 00:04:14,850
each drives about a third of an amp:
that's the ripple.

57
00:04:15,977 --> 00:04:18,173
Since the motor ignores the common mode,

58
00:04:18,213 --> 00:04:21,423
we can add the same signal to all three
references.

59
00:04:21,463 --> 00:04:27,282
Subtract the midpoint of the largest and
smallest: min–max injection.

60
00:04:27,322 --> 00:04:34,475
The peaks flatten, so m can reach 2/√3:
15.5 % more, the full 24 V line-to-line.

61
00:04:34,515 --> 00:04:39,444
On the hexagon, sine-triangle stops at a
circle of V_dc/2;

62
00:04:39,484 --> 00:04:43,957
injection reaches the inscribed circle,
V_dc/√3.

63
00:04:43,997 --> 00:04:49,562
Adding a third harmonic of a quarter of
the fundamental gains 12.2 %.

64
00:04:49,602 --> 00:04:55,058
Lesson 18 shows that space-vector PWM is
the min–max version.

65
00:04:56,158 --> 00:05:00,546
Now the imperfections. Transistors take
time to turn off: switch the upper

66
00:05:00,586 --> 00:05:05,434
one on too early, and both conduct,
shorting the bus. That's shoot-through.

67
00:05:05,474 --> 00:05:08,984
So each turn-on waits a dead time, t_d,
with both switches off.

68
00:05:09,024 --> 00:05:13,160
The current keeps flowing, through a diode
chosen by its direction.

69
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:17,836
Current out of the leg takes the lower
diode: the pulse loses one dead time.

70
00:05:17,876 --> 00:05:22,173
Current into the leg takes the upper
diode: the pulse gains one.

71
00:05:22,213 --> 00:05:27,508
On average, the error is V_dc · t_d ·
f_sw,

72
00:05:27,548 --> 00:05:34,127
against the current: 0.12 V for a typical
250 ns.

73
00:05:35,259 --> 00:05:38,511
Here's the reference motor at 1500 rpm,

74
00:05:38,551 --> 00:05:44,482
simulated with 250 ns of dead time and
open-loop voltage commands.

75
00:05:44,522 --> 00:05:48,398
Each leg's average is off by 0.12 V
against its current,

76
00:05:48,438 --> 00:05:52,994
except near zero crossings, where the
ripple straddles zero.

77
00:05:53,034 --> 00:06:00,005
That small error shrinks the current from
10 to 7.4 A and shifts it 10° ahead:

78
00:06:00,045 --> 00:06:04,720
at 100 Hz, the motor's impedance is only
0.05 Ω.

79
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:07,984
Adding the error back, along the commanded
current,

80
00:06:08,024 --> 00:06:12,684
restores 10 A; a current controller,
Lesson 19, handles the rest.

81
00:06:13,815 --> 00:06:17,029
Your turn: inverter.py in the series code
simulates

82
00:06:17,069 --> 00:06:20,555
the bridge and the reference motor, edge
by edge.

83
00:06:20,595 --> 00:06:25,156
Run the exercise script and check this
lesson's numbers:

84
00:06:25,196 --> 00:06:30,815
2.8 A of ripple, the 10.8 V carrier line,
and the drop to 7.4 A.

85
00:06:30,855 --> 00:06:33,005
Then halve the switching frequency,

86
00:06:33,045 --> 00:06:38,049
or raise the speed until m passes 1, and
compare with min–max injection.

87
00:06:39,175 --> 00:06:44,541
On the bench: a low-voltage inverter
board, a current-limited 24 V supply,

88
00:06:44,581 --> 00:06:48,447
and three 10 Ω resistors in star as a safe
load.

89
00:06:48,487 --> 00:06:54,429
Command m = 0.5 at 50 Hz. Pole a, measured
to the negative rail,

90
00:06:54,469 --> 00:06:59,969
shows 0 and 24 V pulses averaging 12 V
plus a 6 V sine.

91
00:07:00,009 --> 00:07:05,985
Between a and b: +24, 0 and −24 V,
averaging to a 10.4 V peak.

92
00:07:06,025 --> 00:07:11,430
To the resistors' star point: five levels,
6 V peak.

93
00:07:11,470 --> 00:07:14,491
With the motor running, zoom on a rising
pole edge:

94
00:07:14,531 --> 00:07:19,512
when current flows out of the leg, it
waits one dead time for the upper gate.

95
00:07:19,552 --> 00:07:22,318
Safety: the scope's ground clip is
earthed,

96
00:07:22,358 --> 00:07:26,821
and often so is the negative rail, through
a USB debugger; on a pole,

97
00:07:26,861 --> 00:07:31,538
it shorts that leg. Use differential
probes rated above the bus.

98
00:07:32,638 --> 00:07:34,918
Three predictions before next time.

99
00:07:34,958 --> 00:07:39,190
One: 500 ns of dead time at 20 kHz on a 24
V bus.

100
00:07:39,230 --> 00:07:43,828
What's the average error in each leg's
voltage?

101
00:07:44,828 --> 00:07:49,684
Two: how many of the eight states put zero
volts between every pair of terminals?

102
00:07:50,684 --> 00:07:55,475
Three: instead of min–max, add a third
harmonic of one-sixth of the fundamental.

103
00:07:55,515 --> 00:07:58,310
How much more fundamental can you get?

104
00:07:59,310 --> 00:08:03,683
Next time: space-vector PWM and current
sensing — sectors,

105
00:08:03,723 --> 00:08:09,016
dwell times, and why currents are sampled
in the middle of 000.
